column61:
Its beauty (and power) comes with the consistency of the syntax ala Smalltalk / Lisp.<p>PS. Here's an example of creating <i>something</i> similar with Perl:<p><pre><code>    sub IF (&@) {
        my ($block, $flow) = @_;
        [ map { $flow->{$_} || sub {} } qw/else then/ ]->[ $block->() ]->();
    }

    sub THEN (&@) {
        my $block = shift;
        my $flow  = shift || {};
        $flow->{then} = $block;
        $flow;
    }

    sub ELSE (&) {
        my $block = shift;
        +{ else => $block };
    }

    IF { 1 == 0 } THEN { say "True" } ELSE { say "False" };
</code></pre>
And with these being functions and not statements we can also do:<p><pre><code>    my $result = IF { 1 == 1 } THEN { "True" } ELSE { "False" };</code></pre>
